  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist in Kibana.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-1683c2eb Web logs overview\"** (5 panels, all on the `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view)\n- Total requests (count)\n- Average response size (average of `bytes`)\n- Requests over time (line, date histogram on `timestamp`)\n- Responses by status code (bar, terms on `response.keyword`)\n- Top destination countries (pie, terms on `geo.dest`)\n\nI set the dashboard's time range to June 2018 because I assumed that is where the sample data sits. I did not verify it, so check the range if the panels look empty.\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-1683c2eb 5xx spike\"**\n- Type: Elasticsearch query (`.es-query`), searching the data view with the KQL filter `response.keyword : 5*`\n- Runs every 1m, counts matches over a 5m window, fires when the count is greater than 10\n- No connector is attached, so it will evaluate but send no notifications.\n- I enabled it, since you described it as a rule that checks every minute. It was created disabled by default.\n\n**Caveats**\n- I could not run queries against the data directly. The ES|QL endpoint and the Kibana console proxy both returned errors here, so I did not confirm the actual `response` values. The 5xx filter assumes the codes are stored as strings like `503`.\n- The sample data is historical, so the rule will likely never fire on it. It only becomes meaningful once live 5xx traffic is indexed.",
